ie uses the shine to  try to warn her father about the dangers  that await them but since Ellie engages  roles are switched for some reason in  this movie I think this confirms that  gage is now the resident psychic he's  also the one who later has the dream  about pass cow that later sends Rachel  rushing back to Maine to try to stop  Lewis from reburying their daughter so  if you thought Rachel taking a  cross-country flight based on the dream  of a child was a bit much in this movie  she's going back based on the dream of a  two-year-old maybe that's why they  switch to Rachel's family to being from  Chicago to Boston in this one it's a bit  of a shorter trip and perhaps more  believable but before we get to that  part there's a scare with Church  possibly attacking engage in his crib  which leads Louis to release the cat  just before Ellie's ninth birth  I party they invite some of their  friends from Boston to this party and  one of them can be overheard in the  background muttering something about a  big st. Bernard with rabies this is a  reference to another Stephen King book  Cujo about a st. Bernard dog who gets  bit by a bat and turns into a dangerous  beast this reference also exists in  different forms in both the novel and  the 80s movie but there's also a direct  reference to the 80s movie itself and  that the truck driver is listening to a  song called Sheena is a punk rocker and  by the Ramones when he runs over gage  in the new movie he gets distracted by a  phone call from a contact named scheana  right before he hits Ellie Ellie goes  into the coffin wearing white as a  symbol for her purity and childlike  innocence when she later returns as a  zombie she changes into the dirty  clothes that she wore during the  accident signifying that she's now  tainted by the evil influence of the  Windigo when Louis returns from  relocating his daughter's burial place  to the Mi'kmaq grounds I spotted a  height measurement chart for kids in the  Creed's house this might be shown to  make us think of the heights that Ellie  will never reach having met an early  death or it could just be about the fact  that Louis had to dig her up the maximum  height on the chart goes up to 6 feet  actually 6 feet isn't down there it's  more like right here but 6 feet is  typically the depth at which coffins are  buried when Louis wakes up the next day  he hears Ellie once again dancing to the  sugarplum fairy song from The Nutcracker  but this time her gracefulness is gone  and she's just wrecking the house and  basically being a brat meanwhile the  rest of the family is staying with  Rachel's dad in Boston as I mentioned  earlier gage has the shine and he senses  something is wrong a lot of characters  throughout Stephen King's entire body of  work have shining moments but this one  is particularly emphasized by the fact  that gage wakes up from his nap and  starts streaming Pascal over and over in  the same manner that Danny yells out  Redrum in the shining  [Music]  so this dream causes Rachel to drive  back to Maine with gage where she gets  stuck in traffic on the highway on the  side of the road there's a sign saying  that they're 20 miles from the exit for  Derry which is the fictional town in  Stephen King's books most notably the  setting of its insomnia and dreamcatcher  the rest of the movie plays out very  similarly to the original Ellie brings a  scalpel to a gunfight and then somehow  wins and then returns to her own house  trying to kill Rachel at this point in  the movie you can spot a painting of the  Emerald City from The Wizard of Oz  another low-key reference to Oz the  great and terrible it makes sense to see  the painting at this point in the movie  because Oz represents the evil entities  that all the characters are starting to  become when Rachel makes it home and  sees that Lewis has used this cursed  magic to bring back their daughter  she disagrees with the decision because  she realizes that it's not natural  Lewis has his own reason for doing it  then he argues let God take his own care  this is a reference to something Ellie  says near the beginning of the book  after first discovering the pet cemetery  and learning about death the movie ends  with Lewis getting impaled by a cross  and gage being the only one left with  his three zombie family members four if  we count Church coming for him  I think it's implied that he doesn't  survive which to me all right let me  just put my opinion just this one it's  less scary than letting the remaining  child actually survive because when Elie  survives in the original she's gonna be  stuck with all of those scarring  memories and also living with her guy  grandparents which is definitely not a  plus so the credits roll but there's one  more thing that you might have missed  actually I nearly did miss this myself  because they turned off the audio to  begin a Q&amp;A with the casting directors  at my screening but somehow I noticed  this in the credits a cover of Pet  Sematary by the Ramones from a band  called star crawler which is cool  because it's the same end credit music  that Mary Lambert had in her adaptation  of Pet Sematary but also kind of defeats  the purpose because the whole reason  that she got the Ramones was as a  reference to blitzkrieg bop the most  well-known and probably most expensive  to licensed Ramones song which was stuck  in Louis's head throughout the entire  novel that's one of the many things I  talked about in my things you missed  episode on the original Pet Sematary  film so click on the video on the left  if you want to see that remember to  subscribe to see ZZZ world for new  Horrors every week bring that death bell  for notifications and I'll see you in  the next one assuming we both get buried  in a pet cemetery
 

